WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.570
The prison door stood open, the guard had been bought, his friends were ready.

2
00:00:05.570 --> 00:00:14.340
A seventy-year-old man refused to flee and drank the poison — because he believed he still owed something to the law that had handed down a verdict he considered unjust.

3
00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:18.950
So which contract did you sign, that you line up every morning?

4
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:31.600
Long before dawn, in a prison in Athens, a man was sleeping.

5
00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:37.010
He was seventy years old, he would be dead within days, and he was sleeping soundly.

6
00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:43.700
His friend Crito, waiting at the door, sat down amazed at that peaceful sleep and waited for him to wake.

7
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:49.590
He had bribed the guard; the man had opened the door, let him in, and then withdrawn.

8
00:00:49.590 --> 00:00:53.400
So on that morning, the door of that cell stood open.

9
00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.250
The news Crito had brought was urgent.

10
00:00:56.250 --> 00:01:06.460
Every year the Athenians sent a sacred ship to the island of Delos; from the moment that ship set out until the moment it returned, no execution could be carried out in the city.

11
00:01:06.460 --> 00:01:11.870
The few extra weeks Socrates had lived were owed entirely to the slowness of that ship.

12
00:01:11.870 --> 00:01:15.520
Crito said the ship had been sighted off Cape Sounion.

13
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.210
It would be here tomorrow.

14
00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:22.820
If it entered the harbour tomorrow, the hemlock would be drunk the day after.

15
00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:27.590
When Socrates woke, he calmly said the ship would not come that day.

16
00:01:27.590 --> 00:01:35.080
He had had a dream: a beautiful woman dressed in white had called out to him and told him that on the third day he would reach fertile land.

17
00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.810
That was all.

18
00:01:36.810 --> 00:01:42.540
A man postponing the date of his own execution by one day on the strength of a dream.

19
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.950
While Crito's heart was in his mouth, he was talking about the poetry of what he had dreamed.

20
00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:51.280
And why was Socrates there at all?

21
00:01:51.280 --> 00:02:02.610
In the year three hundred and ninety-nine, a popular court made up of more than five hundred citizens had tried him on two charges: not believing in the gods of the city, and corrupting the young.

22
00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:10.580
He had been found guilty by the narrowest of margins; by his own reckoning, had thirty votes gone the other way he would have been acquitted.

23
00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:17.430
The verdict, then, was not some settled truth but the product of the crowd's mood on that particular day.

24
00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.800
Everyone knew this.

25
00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:21.690
Crito knew it too.

26
00:02:21.690 --> 00:02:26.460
And Crito's offer was serious, and what is more, it was ready.

27
00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:32.510
The money was there; Simmias of Thebes had brought it with him, and Cebes would contribute as well.

28
00:02:32.510 --> 00:02:36.960
The guards were cheap, and that part had already been taken care of.

29
00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.570
There were friends waiting for him in Thessaly, where he could live as a foreigner.

30
00:02:41.570 --> 00:02:46.340
The escape had been solved not as an idea but as a piece of logistics.

31
00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:50.790
Only one thing remained: the man had to stand up and walk.

32
00:02:50.790 --> 00:02:53.640
Crito pressed him on three fronts.

33
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.410
The first was personal: if I lose you, I shall never find another friend like you.

34
00:02:58.410 --> 00:03:07.020
The second was shame: people will think we were unwilling to spend money to save you, and we will be remembered as men too miserly to buy back their own friends.

35
00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:17.870
The third was the heaviest, because it concerned the children: your sons are small, you are leaving them fatherless; if you never intended to be a father, if you will not see it through to the end,

36
00:03:17.870 --> 00:03:21.040
then you are choosing the easiest way out.

37
00:03:21.040 --> 00:03:24.850
Socrates' answer was not the answer of a condemned man.

38
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:35.540
As though they were discussing the weather, he put a question to Crito: a man training his body, whose word should he listen to, anyone's at random, or that of the one trainer who knows the craft?

39
00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:39.510
If he listens to the wrong people, he ruins his body.

40
00:03:39.510 --> 00:03:46.040
Very well then; when it comes to justice and injustice, what do we suppose happens to our soul?

41
00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:50.970
The opinion of the crowd is no more an expertise there than it is in the case of the body.

42
00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:57.020
The crowd can kill you, yes; but the crowd cannot make you wise, and it cannot make you right.

43
00:03:57.020 --> 00:04:00.350
That it is able to kill you does not mean it is right.

44
00:04:00.350 --> 00:04:04.960
Then he said the line: what matters is not living, but living well.

45
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:10.370
To live well, to live beautifully, and to live justly are one and the same thing.

46
00:04:10.370 --> 00:04:16.100
So the subject of the argument was never survival; the subject was which life is worth living.

47
00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:21.030
And then he set out a principle that still unsettles a person today.

48
00:04:21.030 --> 00:04:24.520
Under no circumstances should one commit injustice.

49
00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:27.850
Not even when injustice has been done to us.

50
00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:35.020
To return harm for harm is, in the eyes of the people, the most legitimate thing there is, the thing everyone takes for natural.

51
00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:37.550
But Socrates refused it.

52
00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.880
To answer evil with evil is itself an evil.

53
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:49.010
And as he said it he added that those who hold this view are in the minority, and that with those who do not share this principle there is no common ground to stand on.

54
00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.020
He was aware of his own solitude.

55
00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:54.390
Let us stop here and think.

56
00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:56.280
The door is open.

57
00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.930
The bribe has been paid.

58
00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.980
The money is ready.

59
00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:04.510
There is somewhere to go.

60
00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:07.200
And the man does not go.

61
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:14.210
Let us say it once more, in the language of our own lives: all of us have stayed in rooms we could have walked out of.

62
00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:18.340
In jobs we could have left, swallowing words we could have spoken.

63
00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.510
Writing the resignation letter and never sending it.

64
00:05:21.510 --> 00:05:29.800
What happens in Socrates' cell is the purest, barest form of that everyday experience: staying inside when there is not a single chain outside.

65
00:05:29.800 --> 00:05:33.770
But mark this: Socrates was not staying out of fear.

66
00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.780
Nor was he staying out of idleness.

67
00:05:36.780 --> 00:05:43.310
He owed Crito a reason, and in order to give that reason he gave voice to something that was not in that cell at all.

68
00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:47.920
He imagined the laws of Athens coming to him and standing before him.

69
00:05:47.920 --> 00:05:52.530
"Tell us, Socrates," they said, "what do you intend to do?"

70
00:05:52.530 --> 00:05:59.860
The defence the laws mounted in that cell is the first example of one of the longest-lived ideas in Western political thought.

71
00:05:59.860 --> 00:06:01.750
This is what they said.

72
00:06:01.750 --> 00:06:08.760
First they asked a threatening question: can a city stand if the judgments of its courts can be annulled by private individuals?

73
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:12.730
If you run, you alone will have set out to destroy us.

74
00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:21.660
Then they made a claim of kinship: we are the ones who allowed your mother and father to marry, we are the ones who brought you into the world, we raised you and educated you.

75
00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:25.150
We are like your parents, and more than that.

76
00:06:25.150 --> 00:06:30.240
You have no right to strike your father; you have still less right to strike us.

77
00:06:30.240 --> 00:06:33.090
And then they made the decisive move.

78
00:06:33.090 --> 00:06:44.420
In Athens, every citizen, once he comes of age and has seen how the city works and has seen us, the laws, is free, if he does not approve, to gather up his property and go wherever he likes.

79
00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:47.270
Not one of us detains anybody.

80
00:06:47.270 --> 00:06:49.800
You did not do this.

81
00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:56.970
What is more, no one has travelled less than you; you left the city almost only for military campaigns.

82
00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:01.580
You had your children here; so you must have found the place to your liking.

83
00:07:01.580 --> 00:07:07.310
At your trial you could have proposed exile as your penalty, and you did not, you chose death.

84
00:07:07.310 --> 00:07:13.520
Which means that for seventy years, every single day, by choosing to stay, you made an agreement with us.

85
00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:16.850
Not in words, but in deeds.

86
00:07:16.850 --> 00:07:19.540
This is the idea of the contract.

87
00:07:19.540 --> 00:07:24.950
The contract nobody signed, but which you are deemed to have accepted by staying.

88
00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:34.680
For two thousand years this idea stood at the spine of modern political philosophy: the state's authority over you did not descend from the sky, it was born of your consent.

89
00:07:34.680 --> 00:07:39.610
Against claims of absolute power, this was an enormous advance.

90
00:07:39.610 --> 00:07:44.380
Because an authority born of consent is bound by the conditions of that consent.

91
00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:50.430
But there is a problem, and it is the wall that everyone who takes the idea seriously eventually runs into.

92
00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:55.360
In the eighteenth century the Scottish thinker David Hume showed that wall with great clarity.

93
00:07:55.360 --> 00:08:03.010
To tell a poor peasant or artisan, he said, that by remaining in his country he consents to its government is meaningless.

94
00:08:03.010 --> 00:08:09.540
The man knows no other language, has no money to go anywhere, and lives from day to day on what he earns.

95
00:08:09.540 --> 00:08:12.070
His staying is not a choice.

96
00:08:12.070 --> 00:08:22.760
It is like telling a man carried aboard a ship while asleep that by remaining on board he has accepted the captain's authority, when his only alternative is to throw himself into the ocean and drown.

97
00:08:22.760 --> 00:08:25.770
The objection is sharper still today.

98
00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.780
None of us chose the country we were born in.

99
00:08:28.780 --> 00:08:33.710
We did not choose our passport, our tax system, our criminal code.

100
00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:46.480
For most of us, the sentence "if you don't like it, leave" is not a real door but a rhetorical one: for someone without a visa, without money, without the language, without family, it is a door that never opens.

101
00:08:46.480 --> 00:08:51.250
Consent, when the cost of exit is this high, stops being consent.

102
00:08:51.250 --> 00:08:54.900
And this logic is not confined to the state.

103
00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.710
Our daily life is full of contracts we never signed.

104
00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:07.160
Setting up your phone, you tick the box that says "I have read and I accept"; the people who wrote that text know perfectly well you did not read it.

105
00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:15.130
You did not negotiate the clauses of your employment contract, it was placed in front of you and you signed it, because you had rent to pay.

106
00:09:15.130 --> 00:09:20.060
You were not in the room where the decision was taken about how your neighbourhood would be redeveloped.

107
00:09:20.060 --> 00:09:26.750
Every time the same structure: staying counts as consent, when there is no option but to stay.

108
00:09:26.750 --> 00:09:31.200
What is it, then, that holds people together in such a situation?

109
00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:32.610
Fear?

110
00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:42.660
In sixteenth-century France, a young man studying law while still only eighteen years old, Étienne de La Boétie, gave an answer to that question that nobody expected.

111
00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:49.990
In the text known as the Discourse on Voluntary Servitude, he asked: how is it that a tyrant's eyes can see you everywhere?

112
00:09:49.990 --> 00:09:53.320
Are the eyes that watch you not your own eyes?

113
00:09:53.320 --> 00:09:57.610
Are the hands that hold you down not hands taken from your own hands?

114
00:09:57.610 --> 00:10:03.660
One single man crushes millions not with armies of his own, but with the strength those millions lend him.

115
00:10:03.660 --> 00:10:11.150
The conclusion La Boétie drew was frighteningly simple: to bring down a tyrant, there is no need to push him.

116
00:10:11.150 --> 00:10:14.160
It is enough to stop holding him up.

117
00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.730
Like a colossal statue whose base has been pulled away, he collapses under his own weight.

118
00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:24.180
So the real issue is not the power of those who rule, but our own habit.

119
00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:30.870
People remain in servitude not because they have lost their freedom, but because they have forgotten what freedom is.

120
00:10:30.870 --> 00:10:33.560
The question, then, changes.

121
00:10:33.560 --> 00:10:39.930
If nobody signed that contract, and if most of our obedience is habit, should we stop obeying altogether?

122
00:10:39.930 --> 00:10:41.180
No.

123
00:10:41.180 --> 00:10:44.350
Because Socrates himself did not do that.

124
00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:50.080
But Socrates had a line, and once, at the cost of his life, he had drawn it.

125
00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:55.650
When democracy was suspended in Athens, a regime known as the Thirty came to power.

126
00:10:55.650 --> 00:11:04.420
While purging its opponents, this regime put ordinary citizens to work as well; that way everyone's hands were bloodied and nobody could stay clean.

127
00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:12.550
One day they summoned Socrates along with four others and ordered them to arrest a man named Leon of Salamis and bring him in to be executed.

128
00:11:12.550 --> 00:11:14.600
The order was official.

129
00:11:14.600 --> 00:11:19.530
The one who gave it was, at that moment, the effective power in the city.

130
00:11:19.530 --> 00:11:23.500
The penalty for refusal was death, and everyone knew it.

131
00:11:23.500 --> 00:11:26.350
The other four went and brought the man in.

132
00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:28.560
Socrates walked home.

133
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:36.210
Telling the story afterwards, he said that had that regime not fallen soon after, he would in all likelihood have been put to death for it.

134
00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:44.180
So here is what we have before us: the man who refused to escape and the man who refused the order are the same man.

135
00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:47.990
Which means the issue was never obedience as such.

136
00:11:47.990 --> 00:11:50.680
The issue was where obedience ends.

137
00:11:50.680 --> 00:11:57.370
Socrates submitted to the punishment the law imposed, but refused to be the instrument of a man's unjust death.

138
00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:03.100
The difference between the two lies not in who the authority is, but in what is being asked of you.

139
00:12:03.100 --> 00:12:07.230
This distinction does not belong to Greek thought alone.

140
00:12:07.230 --> 00:12:14.720
One of the most often repeated principles of the Islamic tradition says exactly this: obedience is only in what is right and good.

141
00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:18.050
Behind the principle lies a story that is told.

142
00:12:18.050 --> 00:12:22.500
The Prophet sends a detachment out under the command of a certain officer.

143
00:12:22.500 --> 00:12:29.510
On the way the commander grows angry with his soldiers and orders them to gather wood and light a fire, and then to walk into it.

144
00:12:29.510 --> 00:12:32.200
Some of them make to obey.

145
00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:40.490
A young man among them says, "It was precisely to escape the fire that we took refuge with him; do not be hasty." They do not go in.

146
00:12:40.490 --> 00:12:48.780
When they return and recount what happened, the answer they receive is unambiguous: had they entered that fire, they would never have come out of it again.

147
00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.790
Obedience is only in what is good.

148
00:12:51.790 --> 00:12:59.920
The same tradition has a shorter and harder formulation as well: where there is disobedience to the Creator, there is no obedience to the created.

149
00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:03.570
The logic here says something beyond a religious rule.

150
00:13:03.570 --> 00:13:11.220
However high the authority issuing the command, the content of that command is subject to a measure that stands above that authority.

151
00:13:11.220 --> 00:13:17.590
Authority is not absolute, because authority is itself authority only in so far as it serves an end.

152
00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:22.360
One of the figures who turned this idea into a system was al-Farabi.

153
00:13:22.360 --> 00:13:29.210
In his Virtuous City, written in the tenth century, he classifies cities according to the end toward which they are governed.

154
00:13:29.210 --> 00:13:34.940
The virtuous city is virtuous because the one at its head directs its people toward genuine happiness.

155
00:13:34.940 --> 00:13:40.030
Ranged against it are the ignorant cities, the misguided cities, the wicked cities.

156
00:13:40.030 --> 00:13:49.120
What deserves notice is this: in the corrupted cities al-Farabi describes, there is obedience too, and often an impeccable obedience.

157
00:13:49.120 --> 00:13:54.690
There too people carry out orders, order is maintained, the wheels turn.

158
00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:58.500
What is missing is not obedience but the destination.

159
00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:06.630
For al-Farabi, obedience to a ruler takes its legitimacy not from the power in his hands but from the goal toward which he leads his people.

160
00:14:06.630 --> 00:14:10.920
Power does not produce legitimacy; direction does.

161
00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:16.330
Four centuries later Ibn Khaldun looked at the same question with a historian's eye.

162
00:14:16.330 --> 00:14:27.820
In the Muqaddimah he writes that injustice is the harbinger of the ruin of settled, cultivated life, of civilization itself; tyranny does not demonstrate a government's strength, it hastens its collapse.

163
00:14:27.820 --> 00:14:34.030
But Ibn Khaldun's most striking observation for our purposes concerns the education of children.

164
00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:43.440
He describes what becomes of a child raised with violence, with coercion, with beating: the spirit narrows, vitality goes out, the child grows idle,

165
00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:48.050
and out of fear learns to lie and to show the opposite of what he feels within.

166
00:14:48.050 --> 00:14:52.980
And these do not remain passing behaviours in him; they harden into character.

167
00:14:52.980 --> 00:14:59.830
Here Ibn Khaldun is not merely giving advice on pedagogy; he is describing how servility is manufactured.

168
00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:03.800
The submissive human being is not born, but raised.

169
00:15:03.800 --> 00:15:08.570
The twentieth century took this argument out of theory and carried it into the courtroom.

170
00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:20.060
In nineteen forty-five, the founding charter of the international military tribunal established at Nuremberg closed off the defence the accused would reach for most often, before the proceedings had even begun:

171
00:15:20.060 --> 00:15:29.630
having acted on the orders of a government or of a superior does not free a defendant from responsibility; at most it may be taken into account in mitigating the sentence.

172
00:15:29.630 --> 00:15:42.080
In nineteen fifty, in the principles compiled within the United Nations, this was refined further still: having acted under orders does not remove responsibility, provided a moral choice was in fact open to the person.

173
00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:48.290
In drawing a limit around obedience, the law asked precisely our question: could you really have done otherwise?

174
00:15:48.290 --> 00:15:53.380
And the principle did not stay confined to some abstract international text.

175
00:15:53.380 --> 00:16:02.630
The one hundred and thirty-seventh article of the Constitution of the Republic of Turkey sets out what a public official is to do when he finds an order he has received to be unlawful;

176
00:16:02.630 --> 00:16:10.120
if his superior insists on the order and repeats it in writing, the order is carried out and responsibility passes to the one who gave it.

177
00:16:10.120 --> 00:16:20.970
But the same article builds a wall right through its middle: an order whose subject matter constitutes a crime shall under no circumstances be carried out, and whoever carries it out cannot escape responsibility.

178
00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:25.100
The right to hide behind an unlawful order ends exactly there.

179
00:16:25.100 --> 00:16:31.790
Between Socrates walking home rather than to Leon of Salamis and that article of law lie twenty-four centuries.

180
00:16:31.790 --> 00:16:34.320
What they say is the same thing.

181
00:16:34.320 --> 00:16:42.930
On the eleventh of April, nineteen sixty-one, in a courtroom in Jerusalem, a thin, bespectacled man sat inside a bulletproof glass booth.

182
00:16:42.930 --> 00:16:48.020
Adolf Eichmann had run the logistics of transporting millions of people to the death camps.

183
00:16:48.020 --> 00:16:54.390
The defence he mounted at his trial gives this part of our story its title: he was a servant of orders.

184
00:16:54.390 --> 00:16:57.560
He had killed no one with his own hands.

185
00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:03.290
He had drawn up train timetables, calculated quotas, signed correspondence.

186
00:17:03.290 --> 00:17:10.620
What truly shook the thinker Hannah Arendt, who followed the trial, was not Eichmann's monstrousness but the fact that he was not a monster.

187
00:17:10.620 --> 00:17:21.150
What she saw before her was not an ideological fanatic but an ordinary bureaucrat, attached to his title, speaking in clichés, incapable of stepping outside the language of officialdom.

188
00:17:21.150 --> 00:17:30.080
To describe this, Arendt used the phrase the banality of evil, and for years she was criticized for it, because many took it for an attempt to soften the charge.

189
00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:39.170
Her claim was in fact the opposite: what was appalling was that evil on such a scale could be carried out as a job, by an ordinary man who had stopped thinking.

190
00:17:39.170 --> 00:17:52.100
Perhaps the strangest moment of that trial was this: when one of the judges asked Eichmann about his understanding of morality, Eichmann said he had lived his life according to Kant's moral principles,

191
00:17:52.100 --> 00:17:56.550
and then gave a roughly accurate account of the categorical imperative.

192
00:17:56.550 --> 00:18:02.920
He went on to add that once he was given his role in the Final Solution, he had stopped living by that principle.

193
00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:10.410
Arendt showed how Eichmann had twisted Kant: in Kant, the principle demands that every person be a lawmaker through his own reason.

194
00:18:10.410 --> 00:18:16.620
In Eichmann's version, a person was to bring his own will into line with the will of the lawgiver.

195
00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:23.790
The same sentence had been hollowed out and refilled with "whatever the superior wants." Conscience had put on the costume of obedience.

196
00:18:23.790 --> 00:18:33.360
Only three months after the trial in Jerusalem began, in America, at Yale University, a young psychologist named Stanley Milgram placed an advertisement,

197
00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:38.930
and through that newspaper notice gathered ordinary men from New Haven into his laboratory.

198
00:18:38.930 --> 00:18:42.420
They were told this was an experiment on learning and memory.

199
00:18:42.420 --> 00:18:49.110
They would administer electric shocks of increasing intensity to a person in the next room each time he gave a wrong answer.

200
00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:57.080
In reality there was no electricity, and the person in the next room was an actor playing a part, but the subjects did not know this.

201
00:18:57.080 --> 00:19:04.570
The shock switches ran all the way up to four hundred and fifty volts, and the last section of the panel was marked with only three letters.

202
00:19:04.570 --> 00:19:11.420
From the next room came first objections, then shouting, then complaints about his heart, and finally complete silence.

203
00:19:11.420 --> 00:19:22.270
Whenever the subject wanted to stop, the researcher in the lab coat would say, in a calm voice, just one of four sentences: please continue; the experiment requires that you continue;

204
00:19:22.270 --> 00:19:27.680
it is absolutely essential that you continue; you have no other choice, you must go on.

205
00:19:27.680 --> 00:19:29.730
He did not shout.

206
00:19:29.730 --> 00:19:31.780
He did not threaten.

207
00:19:31.780 --> 00:19:34.150
He was simply calm.

208
00:19:34.150 --> 00:19:37.320
Twenty-six out of forty people went to the end.

209
00:19:37.320 --> 00:19:39.850
Sixty-five percent.

210
00:19:39.850 --> 00:19:47.180
But the truly instructive part is the dozens of variations Milgram ran in the years that followed, altering the setup each time.

211
00:19:47.180 --> 00:19:59.470
When the researcher left the room and gave his orders by telephone, obedience collapsed, falling to around twenty percent; and beyond that, some subjects pretended to continue while in fact delivering low shocks.

212
00:19:59.470 --> 00:20:09.520
By contrast, when the subject was not given the job of pressing the switch itself but only an auxiliary task such as reading out the words, while someone else pressed the switch,

213
00:20:09.520 --> 00:20:13.170
obedience leapt to thirty-seven out of forty.

214
00:20:13.170 --> 00:20:16.340
Ninety-two point five percent.

215
00:20:16.340 --> 00:20:19.190
These two numbers show the heart of the matter.

216
00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:27.480
What drives people into obedience is not the harshness of the authority, but the possibility of putting distance between themselves and their own act.

217
00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:36.730
Milgram gave this condition a name: a person stops seeing himself as the author of the deed and begins to see himself as the instrument of someone else's will.

218
00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:48.060
The sentence "I am doing this" quietly turns into "this is being done through me." And the moment that shift takes place, conscience does not fall silent; it simply becomes beside the point.

219
00:20:48.060 --> 00:20:52.510
Today the sentences that bring about that shift are all within easy reach.

220
00:20:52.510 --> 00:20:55.040
The system won't allow it.

221
00:20:55.040 --> 00:20:57.730
That's the procedure.

222
00:20:57.730 --> 00:20:59.940
It came from above.

223
00:20:59.940 --> 00:21:02.310
I just work here.

224
00:21:02.310 --> 00:21:05.000
It's not up to me.

225
00:21:05.000 --> 00:21:07.850
That's what it says on the screen.

226
00:21:07.850 --> 00:21:10.700
The algorithm decided it this way.

227
00:21:10.700 --> 00:21:14.510
We're not meeting our targets.

228
00:21:14.510 --> 00:21:20.240
None of these is a lie; they are usually perfectly true, and that is exactly why they work.

229
00:21:20.240 --> 00:21:25.970
Modern institutions do not abolish responsibility, they thin it out and spread it around.

230
00:21:25.970 --> 00:21:31.700
When you divide a decision among a hundred people, nobody's share is heavy enough to be unbearable.

231
00:21:31.700 --> 00:21:37.270
Everyone is responsible for a small piece, and nobody is responsible for the whole.

232
00:21:37.270 --> 00:21:46.200
The official who signs the eviction order, the clerk who processes the form, the crew that turns up in the morning, each of them has filled in his own box.

233
00:21:46.200 --> 00:21:51.450
But the picture is not hopeless, and the same laboratory showed that too.

234
00:21:51.450 --> 00:22:00.220
In another of Milgram's variations, two more people were placed beside the subject, and at a certain point in the experiment those two stood up and refused.

235
00:22:00.220 --> 00:22:04.830
Of the subjects who saw this, only four out of forty went to the end.

236
00:22:04.830 --> 00:22:11.200
One person saying no, in other words, makes it possible for everyone else in the room to say no.

237
00:22:11.200 --> 00:22:14.690
If obedience is contagious, so is refusal.

238
00:22:14.690 --> 00:22:26.820
In his Ode to Freedom, Namık Kemal says that it is not possible to destroy liberty by cruelty and oppression; to do that, you would have to tear out of humanity its faculty of comprehension, its capacity to understand.

239
00:22:26.820 --> 00:22:31.750
That line points to exactly the place where our discussion of obedience has arrived.

240
00:22:31.750 --> 00:22:39.080
Just as no one can force you to think, no one can force you not to think either; not thinking is a decision that can be taken.

241
00:22:39.080 --> 00:22:43.530
And here we can return to that seventy-year-old man and his open door.

242
00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:47.020
Socrates did not walk out through it.

243
00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:54.030
That same Socrates, on another day, refused to carry out an order he had been given and walked home instead.

244
00:22:54.030 --> 00:23:01.040
These two acts do not contradict one another, because both come from the same source: in each case the decision was his.

245
00:23:01.040 --> 00:23:06.930
He chose to stay and he chose to refuse, and he was ready to pay the price of both.

246
00:23:06.930 --> 00:23:16.980
Obedience in itself is neither a virtue nor a failing; the virtue or the failing lies in whether there is still, behind that obedience, a human being capable of deciding.

247
00:23:16.980 --> 00:23:26.390
So the question is not "do you obey?" Everyone obeys, and has to; a society in which nobody complied with anything would not survive a single day.

248
00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:31.320
The real question is this: can you still name what you are doing in your own words?

249
00:23:31.320 --> 00:23:35.770
When you carry out an order, can you say that it is you who is carrying it out?

250
00:23:35.770 --> 00:23:38.940
Or have you already handed that sentence over?

251
00:23:38.940 --> 00:23:42.430
Because the real danger is not the door being locked.

252
00:23:42.430 --> 00:23:48.320
The real danger is the day when the person who would have noticed the door was open is no longer inside.

253
00:23:48.320 --> 00:23:51.970
Nöron Philosophy'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

254
00:23:51.970 --> 00:23:55.460
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
